Testing each module on its own (**unit testing**) only shows that the module works in isolation, with the test data it was given. The complete system can still fail because:

* the modules do not work together correctly (**integration errors**): the output of one module is not in the format, type or range expected by the next, or parameters are passed in a different order;
* the modules interfere through shared data, shared files or global variables (one module overwrites data used by another), or are called in an unexpected order;
* the system may fail under real conditions that module tests did not reproduce: a large volume of data, many simultaneous users, slow hardware or a different operating environment;
* the design or the requirements were wrong or incomplete (the modules do exactly what they were specified to do, but the specification is wrong).

That is why integration testing and system testing follow unit testing.

